23:03:52 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s an excellent question, and it gets right to the heart of why this mix‑up persists. Those pills aren’t actually **melatonin** at all—they’re **Melanotan** (with an ‘a’). The similar‑sounding name causes a lot of confusion, but they are completely different substances. Here’s the breakdown: ### 1. Melatonin (the sleep hormone) - **What it is:** A natural hormone your brain produces when it gets dark, helping you fall asleep. - **Tanning effect:** None. Taking a melatonin supplement will make you feel drowsy, but it won’t darken your skin. There are no genuine “melatonin tanning pills” on the market. ### 2. Melanotan (the synthetic peptide) - **What it is:** A lab‑made, unlicensed drug originally developed as a sunless tanning agent. There are two main types: Melanotan I and Melanotan II. - **How it actually works:** Melanotan is a synthetic analog of a hormone called **alpha‑melanocyte‑stimulating hormone (α‑MSH)**. This hormone naturally binds to the same **MC1R receptor** I mentioned earlier, but it does so much more powerfully than your body’s normal signals. It directly tells your melanocytes to pump out **eumelanin**, the dark pigment, even without UV exposure. - **The crucial point:** Melanotan II works because it mimics **α‑MSH**—a melanin‑stimulating hormone—not melatonin. The similarity in spelling is purely coincidental. ### 3. Why this confusion “proves” nothing about melatonin If you see a product marketed as a “melatonin tanning pill,” it’s either: - A mislabeled or misspelled product that actually contains Melanotan (often sold illegally online). - A snake-oil scam that mixes actual melatonin with some other unproven ingredients, hoping the name confusion tricks people. The fact that Melanotan can darken skin is simply more evidence that skin colour is controlled by the **melanin** system (melanocytes, MC1R, etc.). It doesn’t connect to the sleep hormone melatonin at all. **Important safety note:** Genuine Melanotan injections and pills are unregulated in most countries, can cause serious side effects (nausea, high blood pressure, new moles, and potentially melanoma), and are not approved as a safe tanning method. If you’ve come across people using “injectable tan” or similar products, that’s what they’re using. So, the existence of these pills accidentally reinforces the correct science: when you artificially stimulate the **melanin** pathway, you get a tan. Melatonin has nothing to do with it.
