23:04:38 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a really logical connection to make, and you’ve put your finger on exactly why this myth feels so “right” to so many people. If darkness triggers melatonin, and tanning makes skin darker, it’s tempting to link the two. But that link breaks down when you look at the biological chain of command: ### 1. Melatonin is a time‑of‑day signal, not a pigment signal When it gets dark, your brain’s pineal gland releases melatonin to tell the rest of your body “it’s nighttime”—cue sleepiness, lower body temperature, etc. It’s a **calendar** message, not a **paint** message. The hormone doesn’t carry any chemical instructions to melanocytes. It just drifts around saying “lights out.” ### 2. Darkness does the opposite of what you’d expect for skin colour Critically, **human skin does not get darker in the dark.** If you spent months in a pitch‑black room, you would not develop a tan—in fact, any existing tan would fade, because the main trigger for melanin production is **ultraviolet (UV) light**, which is absent in darkness. Your body would have no reason to invest energy in extra pigment. So the chain you’re imagining—darkness → melatonin → darker skin—would actually require darkness to **increase** pigmentation. But evolutionarily that would be backwards: melanin exists primarily as a shield against UV damage. Why would you pay to build a shield when there’s no weapon? ### 3. Even in animals where melatonin *does* affect colour, it lightens the skin In the frogs and some lizards I mentioned earlier, melatonin causes pigment cells to contract, making the skin **lighter**, not darker. It’s a camouflage/energy‑saving mechanism: at night, when it’s dark, you don’t need heavy pigmentation, so the skin pales. If that mechanism existed in humans (which it doesn’t), sleeping in total darkness would make you paler—the opposite of a tan. ### 4. The real darkness connection is about UV avoidance What makes the confusion feel “logical” is that we associate darkness with night‑time and sunshine with daytime. But skin colour is driven by the presence or absence of **UV radiation**, not by the ambient light our eyes perceive. You can be out on a brilliantly sunny day and still get no tan if you’re behind glass that blocks UV. Conversely, you can be in a dimly lit sunbed that blasts you with UV‑A and get very, very dark. The darkness of the room is irrelevant. So while the word similarity and the everyday association of “dark” with “darkness” make the idea very attractive, the biological reality is that the sleep hormone melatonin and the skin pigment melanin are as unrelated as a parking ticket and a suitcase—they just happen to look alike on paper.
